(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-pulmonary-vascular-disorders-vignettes-version-2"]={"title":"Pulmonary Vascular Disorders Vignettes 2 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 67-year-old man with heart failure takes metoprolol succinate at home. He develops flash pulmonary edema after a hypertensive surge (blood pressure 196/108 mmHg), with severe dyspnea and diffuse crackles, but he is warm, well perfused and not in shock. Which step should be avoided?","choices":["Sitting him upright, legs dangling","Starting an intravenous nitrate","Giving intravenous furosemide","Stopping his oral beta-blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In flash pulmonary edema, oral beta-blockers are continued unless the patient is in shock. He is hypertensive, warm and well perfused, so stopping his metoprolol is the step to avoid while the acute measures work. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 46"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman has 2 days of mild pleuritic chest pain. Her Wells score is 0, and the clinician's gestalt for pulmonary embolism is very low. Her heart rate is 84/min and oxygen saturation 98%. She has no hemoptysis, takes no estrogen, has no prior clots, has had no surgery or trauma and has no leg swelling. Applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), which step fits?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Order a D-dimer","Stop: no further testing","Computed tomographic angiography"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. She fails one criterion, age under 50. A patient with a Wells score of 4 or less who fails any rule-out criterion goes to a D-dimer: a negative result rules embolism out, and a positive one leads to imaging. She passes every other item. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"A 54-year-old woman collapses 6 days after a cesarean delivery. She is pale and sweating, her heart rate is 128/min, her blood pressure is 78/48 mmHg and her neck veins are distended. Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography shows a large central pulmonary embolism, and echocardiography shows an acutely dilated right ventricle with the septum bowing toward the left ventricle. What best explains her hypotension?","choices":["Left ventricular contractile failure","Right ventricular wall hypertrophy","Widespread systemic vasodilation","Reduced left ventricular filling"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A massive embolism spikes right ventricular wall tension, and the right ventricle dilates acutely. The septum bows leftward into the left ventricle, so left ventricular diastolic filling falls, output plummets and systemic hypotension follows. Her distended neck veins and septal shift trace that chain. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 34, 50"},{"q":"A 38-year-old woman has been bedbound at home for 7 days with severe influenza and now has sudden dyspnea. The clinician judges pulmonary embolism more likely than any alternative diagnosis, giving a Wells score of 4.5. Her heart rate is 92/min and oxygen saturation 97%, and she has no hemoptysis, no estrogen use, no prior clots, no surgery or trauma and no leg swelling, so she would meet every PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria) item. Her kidney function is normal and she has no contrast allergy. Which step fits?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan first","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","Stop: she meets every rule-out item","A D-dimer, then imaging if positive"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The rule-out criteria come after the Wells score and are used when it is 4 or less. Her score of 4.5 (embolism more likely than alternatives 3 + immobilization for more than 3 days 1.5) makes embolism likely, so the D-dimer and the rule-out criteria are bypassed and she goes directly to computed tomographic pulmonary angiography. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is cyanotic and edematous, and his arterial blood gas shows hypoxemia with hypercapnia (carbon dioxide 58 mmHg, normal 35-45), the 'blue bloater' picture. He has a chronic unproductive cough and progressive exertional dyspnea. Examination shows a parasternal heave, an intensified P2, tender hepatomegaly, ascites and dependent pitting edema. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Left ventricular failure","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Chronic cor pulmonale","Acute cor pulmonale"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Years of global alveolar hypoxia and hypercapnia in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease raise pulmonary resistance and right ventricular afterload. The hypertrophied right ventricle eventually decompensates: a parasternal heave, an intensified P2, tender hepatomegaly, ascites and dependent pitting edema are the signs of that chronic failure. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 6, 9, 11, 23, 35"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has chronic cor pulmonale. His electrocardiogram shows peaked P waves, severe right axis deviation and a tall R wave in lead V1. Which chamber change does the tall R wave in V1 indicate?","choices":["Left ventricular hypertrophy","Left atrial enlargement","Right ventricular hypertrophy","Right atrial enlargement"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A tall R wave in V1 is the electrocardiogram sign of right ventricular hypertrophy, the thickened chamber of chronic cor pulmonale. His peaked P waves point to right atrial enlargement, and severe right axis deviation goes with the chronic disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 14"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has severe obstructive sleep apnea and chronic obstructive pulmonary disease, with nocturnal hypoxemia. Echocardiography shows a normal left ventricle and an enlarged right ventricle, and his ventilation-perfusion scan is normal. Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group fits?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung disease and hypoxia make up Group 3, and both of his conditions are on that list: chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and sleep apnea, with its chronic nocturnal alveolar hypoventilation. Alveolar hypoxia constricts the pulmonary vessels and loads his right ventricle. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 5, 6, 19"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman is 5 days out from recent spine surgery when she collapses with a massive pulmonary embolism. Her blood pressure has been 72/44 mmHg for 30 minutes. Which intervention fits?","choices":["Catheter-directed thrombolysis","Anticoagulation alone, then reassess","Mechanical thrombectomy or embolectomy","Full-dose systemic thrombolysis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Thrombolysis is the standard for massive, hemodynamically unstable embolism, but recent surgery is an absolute contraindication to it. Mechanical thrombectomy or surgical embolectomy, which physically extracts or macerates the clot, is reserved for exactly this patient: massive embolism with a contraindication to thrombolytics. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 60"},{"q":"A 63-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has ankle edema, ascites and a right ventricular S3 gallop. Her symptoms have worsened rapidly over 3 months, she has fainted several times, she is short of breath at rest (World Health Organization functional class IV), and her six-minute walk distance is 140 m. Which prognostic risk band fits her?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Signs of right ventricular failure (edema, ascites, a right ventricular S3) place her in the high-risk row, and every other feature agrees: rapid progression, repeated syncope, class IV and a walk under 165 m. High risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality above 10%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 22, 25"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with cor pulmonale from interstitial lung disease has ankle edema and mild ascites. Her blood pressure is 112/70 mmHg and her potassium is 4.1 mEq/L (normal 3.5-5.0). A loop diuretic is started. Which approach to her diuresis should be avoided?","choices":["Checking potassium during diuresis","Aggressive, rapid volume removal","Careful, monitored diuresis","Adding spironolactone to the loop"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The damaged right ventricle relies on elevated filling pressure (preload) to keep output up. Removing volume too aggressively drops that preload and starves the left ventricle, and output can fall catastrophically. Careful, monitored diuresis treats her edema without crashing her output. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 16, 27"},{"q":"A 46-year-old woman with pulmonary arterial hypertension has no signs of right ventricular failure and no syncope, and her symptoms have progressed slowly over 2 years. She is in World Health Organization functional class II, and her six-minute walk distance is 470 m. Which prognostic risk band fits her?","choices":["Low risk, under 5% at 1 year","Low risk, 5-10% at 1 year","Intermediate risk, 5-10% at 1 year","High risk, over 10% at 1 year"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Every feature sits in the low-risk row: no right ventricular failure signs, slow progression, no syncope, functional class II and a six-minute walk distance above 440 m. Low risk carries an estimated 1-year mortality under 5%. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"A 48-year-old man collapses in his kitchen 9 days after knee arthroscopy. His blood pressure is 74/46 mmHg, he is cold and clammy, and his neck veins are distended. Which embolism syndrome does this presentation represent?","choices":["Pleuritic pain from distal infarction","Isolated dyspnea syndrome","Silent, asymptomatic embolism","Collapse from massive proximal clot"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Syncope, severe hypotension and obstructive shock form the circulatory collapse syndrome, which reflects massive, proximal occlusion of the pulmonary arteries. His distended neck veins show the acutely failing right ventricle behind the clot. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 48, 50"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man had a pulmonary embolism 10 weeks ago, 2 weeks after knee ligament surgery. He has no cancer, no known clotting disorder and no other risk factors, and he is doing well on anticoagulation. He asks how long he will need it. Which response fits?","choices":["Consider stopping at 3 months","Indefinite anticoagulation","Stop it now, at 10 weeks","Continue for at least 5 years"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. His embolism was provoked by a transient risk factor, recent surgery, so stopping anticoagulation can be considered at the 3-month decision point. Persistent or unprovoked causes, such as active malignancy or a hypercoagulable state, are the ones that favor indefinite therapy. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with pneumonia develops septic shock. Over 24 hours he becomes severely hypoxemic with bilateral alveolar infiltrates. Echocardiography shows normal ventricular function, and a pulmonary artery catheter shows a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg. How is his pulmonary edema classified?","choices":["Cardiogenic, a pressure problem","Noncardiogenic, from high wedge pressure","Cardiogenic, from diastolic stiffening","Noncardiogenic, a permeability leak"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Sepsis causes systemic vasodilation and an endothelial leak, so protein-rich fluid crosses a shattered barrier: a permeability problem, the pipe is leaking. His normal wedge pressure and normal ventricular function rule out a hydrostatic, cardiogenic cause. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 34, 46"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman in the intensive care unit has septic acute kidney injury (creatinine 3.1 mg/dL, normal 0.6-1.1). She develops sudden hypoxemia and a swollen right leg, pulmonary embolism is judged likely, and she is too unstable to leave the unit. Which imaging fits her?","choices":["Transthoracic echocardiography alone","Portable bedside perfusion scan","Computed tomographic pulmonary angiography","A D-dimer to decide on imaging"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The ventilation-perfusion approach is best for renal dysfunction, severe contrast allergy and critically ill patients, who can have a portable bedside perfusion scan. Her kidney injury argues against contrast, and her instability argues against travel to a scanner. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 55, 56"},{"q":"A 70-year-old woman with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale has an arterial oxygen tension of 52 mmHg (normal 80-100) on room air. Her cough and wheeze are at baseline, and her wedge pressure is normal. Which therapy targets the main driver of her pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Continuous supplemental oxygen","Aggressive loop diuresis","Oral calcium channel blocker","Oral endothelin antagonist"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alveolar hypoxia is the single most important driver of pulmonary hypertension in chronic obstructive pulmonary disease: global hypoxia causes diffuse pulmonary vasoconstriction and a relentless rise in right ventricular afterload. For Group 3 disease, continuous supplemental oxygen is paramount because it reverses that hypoxic vasoconstriction. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 6, 15, 16, 28"},{"q":"An 84-year-old woman with heart failure with preserved ejection fraction is admitted with acute pulmonary edema. Her blood pressure is 148/82 mmHg and her creatinine is 1.1 mg/dL (normal 0.6-1.1). Echocardiography shows a stiff, thick left ventricle with a high end-diastolic pressure. Which diuresis strategy fits her?","choices":["Withholding diuretics entirely","Morphine before any diuretic","Gentle, precise diuresis","Aggressive, rapid diuresis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Elderly patients usually have heart failure with preserved ejection fraction from age-related myocardial stiffening, with a high end-diastolic pressure and extreme sensitivity to preload. Their therapeutic margin is narrow, so diuresis must be gentle and precise. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 39, 42"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man has 3 days of mild pleuritic chest pain. His Wells score is 1.5 (heart rate 104/min). His oxygen saturation is 97%, and he has no hemoptysis, no estrogen use, no prior clots, no recent surgery or trauma and no leg swelling. Because he fails the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), a D-dimer is sent, and it is 310 ng/mL (normal under 500 ng/mL). Which step fits?","choices":["Repeat the D-dimer in 1 week","No imaging; embolism is excluded","Computed tomographic angiography","Ventilation-perfusion scan"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. He fails two criteria (age 58 and heart rate 104/min), so a D-dimer was right with a Wells score of 4 or less. The D-dimer has high sensitivity, and a negative result safely excludes embolism in this low-probability outpatient, so no imaging is needed. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 57"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with pulmonary arterial hypertension, functional class II, asks how to protect his health between visits. His resting oxygen saturation is 96%, and it falls to 87% when he walks briskly. Which recommendation should be avoided?","choices":["Supervised aerobic rehabilitation","Oxygen during exertion and sleep","Annual influenza immunization","Strict bed rest to save energy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strict bed rest should be avoided in pulmonary hypertension: supervised, steady aerobic rehabilitation is safe, prevents muscle deconditioning and improves fatigue. Resting to spare the heart only adds deconditioning to his disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has an acute anterior myocardial infarction and develops severe dyspnea with bilateral crackles. A pulmonary artery catheter shows a wedge pressure of 26 mmHg. Which force is driving fluid into her alveoli?","choices":["Low plasma oncotic pressure","A shattered capillary barrier","High plasma oncotic pressure","High hydrostatic pressure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her infarcted left ventricle fails, and pressure backs up into the pulmonary capillaries: elevated capillary hydrostatic pressure is the outward force and the engine of cardiogenic edema. The capillary membrane is intact but overwhelmed, and her wedge pressure of 26 mmHg confirms a pressure problem. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 38"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a confirmed large pulmonary embolism has an electrocardiogram showing a deep S wave in lead I and a Q wave with an inverted T wave in lead III (S1Q3T3). What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["Severe right ventricular strain","A sensitive screen for embolism","An inferior myocardial infarction","Left ventricular hypertrophy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. S1Q3T3 suggests severe right ventricular strain rather than embolism as such: it is highly specific but has low sensitivity. In a patient with a large embolism, it reflects the acutely loaded right ventricle. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 58-year-old man with heart failure with reduced ejection fraction takes all four classes of quadruple therapy at maximal tolerated levels. He is breathless at rest and has been hospitalized four times in 6 months. Which step fits now?","choices":["Stage C maintenance unchanged","Evaluation for advanced therapies","Add a fifth oral drug class","Continue and recheck in a year"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Marked symptoms at rest despite maximal therapy, with frequent hospitalizations, define stage D heart failure. Stage D calls for immediate evaluation for advanced therapies: heart transplantation, mechanical circulatory support such as a left ventricular assist device, or palliative and hospice care. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 41, 44"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman develops sudden severe dyspnea 8 days after a hip fracture repair, and computed tomographic pulmonary angiography shows a massive pulmonary embolism. Her blood pressure is 84/52 mmHg and her heart rate 132/min. Which bedside finding is expected from her acutely dilated right ventricle?","choices":["Bibasilar crackles","Slow, bounding pulse","Distended neck veins","Tender hepatomegaly"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When the right ventricle dilates acutely, blood backs up behind it and the neck veins distend; a stretched tricuspid annulus adds a regurgitant murmur. Tachycardia and pallor complete the acute picture, which is what her massive embolism and low blood pressure predict. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 10, 11, 23"},{"q":"A 69-year-old woman with heart failure has acute dyspnea. Her chest film shows central perihilar opacities, thickened, blurred bronchiole walls and short horizontal lines at the lung periphery that reach the pleura. What are the short horizontal peripheral lines called?","choices":["Peribronchial cuffing","Westermark sign","Hampton's hump","Kerley B lines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Kerley B lines are short horizontal lines at the lung periphery, made by fluid engorging the interlobular septa. With central perihilar (batwing) opacities and peribronchial cuffing, they complete the chest film of cardiogenic pulmonary edema. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 37, 51"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman has exertional dyspnea, ankle edema and a loud P2, and echocardiography shows an enlarged right ventricle with a normal left ventricle. She has smoked for 30 years. Which step comes first in finding the cause of her cor pulmonale?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan first","Pulmonary artery angiography","Chest x-ray and lung function tests","Right heart catheterization first"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The workup runs from common to rare: step 1 is a chest film and pulmonary function tests to look for parenchymal lung disease (Group 3), such as hyperinflation or an obstructive pattern from chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Only if those are normal does the workup move on to a scan for clot and then to catheterization. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension remains in functional class IV despite maximal medical therapy, including a continuous intravenous prostanoid. Which referral offers the only definitive option?","choices":["Lung transplantation evaluation","Pulmonary thromboendarterectomy","Mitral valve replacement","Left ventricular assist device"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For pulmonary arterial hypertension refractory to maximal medical therapy (or pulmonary veno-occlusive disease), lung transplantation is the only definitive option, with continuous intravenous support as a bridge. Early palliative care discussions belong alongside the referral. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 19, 29, 44"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man had a large anterior myocardial infarction 3 years ago and has heart failure with an ejection fraction of 25%. He now has ankle edema and distended neck veins, and echocardiography shows a dilated right ventricle. Right heart catheterization shows an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a pulmonary capillary wedge pressure of 27 mmHg. How is his right-sided failure classified?","choices":["Group 2 pulmonary hypertension","Cor pulmonale from lung disease","Group 1 arterial hypertension","Group 4 thromboembolic disease"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A wedge pressure of 27 mmHg shows high left-sided filling pressure, and his failing left ventricle (ejection fraction 25%) is pushing pressure back into the lungs. Left heart disease, including heart failure with reduced ejection fraction, is World Health Organization Group 2, the passive post-capillary group, and Group 2 is not cor pulmonale. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 4, 19"},{"q":"A 50-year-old man with idiopathic pulmonary arterial hypertension is not vasoreactive. Over 2 months he has declined rapidly and is now breathless at rest with edema, functional class IV. Which therapy fits him?","choices":["Continuous intravenous prostanoid","High-dose oral calcium channel blocker","Oral phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor alone","Strict bed rest until stable"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. A patient who is not vasoreactive and is high risk (class IV or rapid clinical decline) needs continuous intravenous or subcutaneous parenteral prostanoids such as epoprostenol. Prostacyclins are the most potent class for vasodilation and are essential for severe disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 26, 28"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man has a small pulmonary embolism on computed tomographic pulmonary angiography, with a normal right ventricle. His blood pressure is 132/84 mmHg, his troponin I is under 0.01 ng/mL (normal under 0.04), and his B-type natriuretic peptide is 40 pg/mL (normal under 100). How is his embolism risk-stratified, and what follows for treatment?","choices":["Massive; systemic thrombolysis","Low risk; no treatment needed","Low risk; standard anticoagulation","Submassive; anticoagulate, monitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Normotension, no right ventricular dysfunction and negative cardiac biomarkers define low-risk embolism. It carries an excellent prognosis and is treated with standard anticoagulation, which halts clot propagation while the body's own fibrinolysis works. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 59"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has abdominal bloating, early satiety, profound fatigue and an episode of syncope while climbing stairs. His lungs are clear on auscultation. His chest film shows enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning, and right heart catheterization shows an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pulmonary embolism","Right heart failure","Left heart failure","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bloating, early satiety, profound fatigue and exertional syncope with clear lungs are right heart failure. Enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning, and a normal wedge pressure with a high mean pulmonary artery pressure, confirm a pre-capillary, right-sided process. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 23, 35, 50"}]};
